<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Birchos HaRe'iyah [Blessings of Seeing]<br>v'Sha'ar Brachos Pratiyos [and Other Specific Blessings]<br>Halachah 5, Part 3 (§§ 14–20)<br><span style="font-size:.95rem;font-weight:400">Based on <span class="lr">LM I, "Vayhi MiKetz — Zikaron"</span></span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 14 — <i>Hatavas Chalom</i> (Dream Amelioration): The Power of Speech</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="h">Hatavas Chalom</span> [dream amelioration]. One who dreamed a bad dream should have it ameliorated before three people who love him. As explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: sleep is the aspect of general memory of the World to Come — during sleep the soul ascends to the World to Come. One who dreamed a bad dream — this is a sign that the memory has been flawed in some aspect; he did not merit cleaving his soul properly during sleep to the World to Come. Had he merited ascending during sleep to the World to Come as proper, his dreams would certainly have been good and settling — for there in the World to Come everything is good. A bad dream thus comes from flawed memory.</div>

<div class="p">The flaw of memory comes through the evil eye or through the flaw of the imaginative faculty [<span class="h">m'dameh</span>], as explained there — through which the dream is flawed. (The imaginative faculty is the aspect of the dream — the aspect of "Pharaoh's dream is one"; see there.) Therefore before sleep we request the tikun of the eyes and the tikun of the imaginative faculty: <span class="mx">"illuminate my eyes lest I sleep the death"</span> — to be saved from the evil eye (= the side of death, the death of the heart, as explained there). And: <span class="mx">"Who illuminates the pupil of the eye"</span> — Hashem illuminates the eye in His goodness, to save it from the evil eye — especially needed during sleep, to guard the memory (= the soul's ascent to the World to Come). And: <span class="mx">"let not my thoughts, evil dreams, and evil imaginings disturb me"</span> — a request to be saved from the flaw of the imaginative faculty, the source of all terrifying thoughts, evil dreams, and evil imaginings. Before sleep we request to be saved from the evil eye and the flawed imaginative faculty — to merit the memory during sleep: cleaving one's thought to the World to Come.</div>

<div class="p">When one dreamed a bad dream (= the memory was flawed), he must have it ameliorated before specifically three people who love him. Love is the opposite of the evil eye (which hates everyone); love is also the tikun of the imaginative faculty (which comes from <span class="h">lashon hara</span>, causing fallen loves, as explained there). Holy love among Israel is the opposite of this. Through those who love him, the dream is repaired — they have the power through holy love to ameliorate the dream, subdue the evil eye and the flawed imaginative faculty, and turn the dream to good — repairing the flawed memory.</div>

<div class="p">The essential amelioration is through speech — <span class="mx">"all dreams follow the mouth."</span> Speech has exceedingly great power, as we see from this very institution: the Sages established that the dream is ameliorated through speech. Three Jews say that the dream is good — <span class="h">chalmah tavah chazisa</span> [you have seen a good dream] — and through this the dream is truly turned to good. Though he dreamed a bad dream, since three people say with full voice that it is good, repeating it several times, and adding three <span class="h">hafuchos</span> [reversals], <span class="h">sh'lomos</span> [assurances of peace], and <span class="h">p'duyos</span> [redemptions] — through this the dream is truly transformed from bad to good.</div>

<div class="p">Speech has exceedingly great power to transform a person from bad to good. The essential victory of war is through speech — the essential weapon of Israel: <span class="mx">"our power is only in the mouth."</span> This is the essential weapon of <span class="h">Mashiach</span> (= all the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>, in whom all Israel are bound and encompassed). <span class="h">Mashiach</span>'s entire arsenal is solely through <span class="h">tefilah</span> (= speech), as explained (<span class="lr">LM I:2</span>) and in many places. Through this the evil eye (= forgetfulness) is essentially subdued. Nullifying forgetfulness and meriting memory is through speech: <span class="mx">"remember — with the mouth"</span> — as we see tangibly: through reviewing one's learning many times with full voice, one remembers it always.</div>

<div class="p">So too for the essential memory — remembering the World to Come always, against which the evil eye (= forgetfulness) attacks to erase this memory from the heart, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. The remedy: to repeat this memory through speech — accustoming oneself every day to speak with full voice about this memory, speaking to one's heart and reminding oneself with full voice: "What will my end be in the World to Come? What will become of me? Did I come here to establish myself, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, in this world? Everyone knows there is no permanence in this passing, fleeting, flying world. I came here only to prepare myself for the World to Come." For this reason one must study <span class="h">mussar</span> books founded on the words of our Sages in the Gemara, Midrashim, and the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span>.</div>

<div class="p">[Not their books which they call <span class="h">mussar</span> books founded on the philosophies of the investigators — though at first they cover their words in some truth, saying that time flies and there is no purpose in this world, afterward they reverse the approach and say the purpose is to spend one's days on their wisdoms and languages. Through their evil <span class="h">mussar</span> specifically, they trap souls to fly to <span class="h">Gehinnom</span> and the lowest pit — for their entire intention is for evil, to divert from the true path we received from our holy Sages in the Gemara and Midrashim.]</div>

<div class="p">All true <span class="h">mussar</span> books mention the purpose of the World to Come. One must also do <span class="h">hisbodedus</span> [secluded prayer] every day, speaking with full voice about the purpose of the World to Come. Speech has great power to remind a person. This is: <span class="v">"whenever I speak of Him, I surely remember Him"</span> (as explained elsewhere).</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of <span class="mx">"up to his nose"</span> mentioned there — the essential subduing of the evil eye is through the kingdom of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>, called <span class="h">chotem</span> [nose/seal]: <span class="v">"the breath of our nostrils — the anointed of Hashem."</span> The essential power of the true holy kingdom (= the kingdom of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>) is through speech: <span class="h">malchus peh</span> [kingship is the mouth]. A king governs his entire kingdom through speech; all the victory of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>'s holy kingdom is through speech.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the entire Book of <span class="h">Tehilim</span>, composed by David HaMelech <span class="h">alav hashalom</span> — the root of the kingdom of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>. He fought Hashem's wars all his days, physically and spiritually, winning them all through speech — through countless <span class="h">tefilos</span> beyond measure, encompassing them all through his holy spirit in the Book of <span class="h">Tehilim</span>. Therefore he truly merited the eternal kingdom — the kingdom of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>. <span class="h">LaMenatzeach</span> [To the Victor] — which opens most chapters of <span class="h">Tehilim</span> — speaks of this: <span class="h">Tehilim</span> (= <span class="h">tefilos</span>, songs, and praises = the totality of holy speech of the memory of the World to Come, when we will be engaged solely in thanking and praising Him; as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"whoever says <i>Tehilim</i> in this world merits saying it in the World to Come"</span>). The essential saying of <span class="h">Tehilim</span> (= all holy speech for the memory of the World to Come) must be with <span class="h">LaMenatzeach</span> — with great victory, like a warrior in battle who must not lose heart. Among the officers of war are many teachings about strengthening the soldiers' hearts during battle. Exactly so here: one must strengthen oneself greatly at all times, knowing and believing that however things stand, speech has exceedingly great power — the essential victory is through speech. <span class="h">LaMenatzeach</span> — mentioned before each <span class="h">mizmor</span> — is as if saying, reminding, and warning every person who wishes to enter Hashem's war (for which alone he was created): whoever wants to be victorious in this war — let him say this <span class="h">mizmor</span> or that <span class="h">mizmor</span> with full voice. This is <span class="h">LaMenatzeach</span> — understand.</div>

<div class="p">He also strengthens himself through his own speech, saying of himself: <span class="h">LaMenatzeach mizmor l'David</span> — this <span class="h">mizmor</span> is for David, who is the victor over all through his holy speech. One must strengthen oneself greatly when engaging in <span class="h">tefilah</span>, conversation, and speech between himself and his Creator — and believe that he will certainly win through this, if he is strong in this all his life until the end, until his soul departs, however things may be. This is called a true victor — for however things are, he has already won a great deal through his holy speech to Hashem. Every movement of holy victory endures forever — especially the speech and voice of <span class="h">tefilah</span>, the most precious of all. <span class="v">"They shall speak of the glory of Your kingdom, and talk of Your might"</span> — the essential glory of His kingdom is drawn through speech. <span class="v">"They shall speak of Your might"</span> — the essential <span class="h">Gevuros</span> of Hashem (= the victory of Hashem's wars: <span class="v">"Hashem is mighty and a hero; Hashem is a hero of war"</span>) are drawn specifically through speech.</div>

<div class="p">Through speech, the kingdom of <span class="h">Mashiach</span> is drawn — subduing the evil eye and meriting memory. The essential memory is through speech. This is <span class="h">Hatavas Chalom</span> through speech: <span class="mx">"all dreams follow the mouth"</span> (as brought in the Gemara — many wondrous stories of how the words of the interpreters were fulfilled, for good or otherwise). Speech has great power — through it the memory is repaired (= the essential tikun of the dream).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore the dream is ameliorated before three people — corresponding to the three aspects (man, place, and time) upon which the memory depends. The three <span class="h">hafuchos</span> [reversals] and three <span class="h">p'duyos</span> [redemptions] — all correspond to the three aspects through which the memory is repaired, transforming the dream to good, <span class="h">amain</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 15 — <i>Birchas Kohanim</i> and <i>Adir BaMarom</i>: The Good Eye Repairs the Dream</div>

<div class="p">This is why our Sages say: if one has no one to ameliorate his dream, he should say during <span class="h">Birchas Kohanim</span> [the Priestly Blessing]: <span class="mx">"<i>Adir bamarom Hashem</i>..."</span> The <span class="h">Kohanim</span> are the aspect of holy love — <span class="mx">"the <i>Kohain</i> shall take a fistful"</span> (as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>). The <span class="h">Kohanim</span> are also the aspect of the good eye: <span class="v">"the good-eyed one shall be blessed [<i>y'vorach</i>]"</span> — this is <span class="h">Birchas Kohanim</span>. During <span class="h">Birchas Kohanim</span> — when the essential drawing of "the good-eyed one shall bless" occurs through the <span class="h">Kohanim</span> (= holy love) — the dream is repaired (= the memory is repaired), drawn through these tikunim: the good eye and holy love.</div>

<div class="p">Therefore one says then: <span class="h">Adir bamarom</span> — the aspect of: <span class="v">"more than the sounds of mighty waters, the majestic breakers of the sea — majestic on high is Hashem."</span> He always wins — <span class="v">"You, Hashem, are exalted forever"</span>; <span class="mx">"Your hand is always on top."</span> By mentioning <span class="h">Adir bamarom</span> for the dream's tikun: You who are majestic on high, always winning — no evil eye has dominion or power against You. Therefore I beseech You: place life and blessing and peace — nullifying the dispute, hatred, and provocation of the evil eye that flaws the memory (= the source of the dream's corruption). Rather, draw upon us life and blessing as a safeguard of peace — the opposite of the evil eye (which causes all dispute, being the side of death and not desiring blessing). Draw upon us life and blessing — drawn from the good eye: <span class="v">"the good-eyed one shall be blessed"</span> (= life): <span class="v">"for there Hashem commanded the blessing — life."</span> For You are majestic on high, dwelling in might, and You always win: <span class="v">"more than the sounds of mighty waters — majestic on high is Hashem."</span></div>

<div class="sn">§ 16 — Educating Youth in Torah: <i>"From the Mouths of Babes"</i></div>

<div class="p">This is the great <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of educating youth in Torah — whose essence is through speech, as our Sages expound on: <span class="v">"you shall teach them to your children, to speak in them"</span> — <span class="mx">"when a child can speak, his father must train him to speak in the Holy Tongue and teach him words of Torah: '<i>Torah tzivah lanu</i>' and '<i>Shma Yisrael</i>.'"</span> Speech has great power. Even a child who does not understand what he says — the words of Torah he is trained to speak are exceedingly precious and it is a great <span class="h">mitzvah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">From this comes a rebuke to those who mock the people of truth who draw youth to Hashem's service from their young years. On the contrary, the essential <span class="h">mitzvah</span> is specifically to draw the youth — they are still at the beginning of the war and the victory. Everything they are taught and trained in (some holiness of Torah, <span class="h">tefilah</span>, and good deeds) is exceedingly precious — everything done now in their youth endures forever (holy victory, however small, endures eternally). Through this they are trained from youth to fight Hashem's wars and walk in straight paths: <span class="v">"train the youth according to his way; even when he grows old, he will not depart from it."</span> The Torah instructs us to teach even a child who can merely speak — how much more so youth who are already of <span class="h">mitzvah</span> age and don <span class="h">tefillin</span>! Certainly there is a great obligation to speak with them about <span class="h">yiras shamayim</span> every day, training them in the holy path specifically from their youth — because later, the older they grow, the harder it is to draw them close, having already strayed far from the path, as Rabbainu <span class="h">zichrono livrachah</span> writes on: <span class="v">"I have strayed like a lost sheep."</span></div>

<div class="p">In truth, this opposition to the youth derives from the angels' complaint against man: <span class="mx">"what is man that You remember him?"</span> — the source of the <span class="h">Satan</span>'s hold (he was among the complainers, as brought). From there descend the words spoken against those who engage in truly drawing people to Hashem's service.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"Hashem our Master, how majestic is Your Name in all the earth — You Who placed Your splendor upon the heavens"</span> — the complaint of the angels: humans are unworthy of receiving such holy, lofty Torah; it should be <span class="v">"placed upon the heavens."</span> But the truth is otherwise — Hashem specifically desires the service of the lowly, clods of earth lacking understanding. He specifically desires the service of children and youth — babes and sucklings. As the next verse immediately explains: <span class="v">"from the mouths of babes and sucklings You have established strength, for the sake of Your adversaries — to silence the enemy and the avenger."</span> Specifically from the mouths of babes — their speech is exceedingly precious in His eyes. From it He establishes a foundation of strength — specifically through this He wins against the adversaries, silencing the enemy and avenger. The essential victory is through speech — specifically the speech of babes and sucklings, because they are still in their youth with the blood boiling, where the essential victory lies (the essential victory is in the blood, as explained elsewhere [<span class="lr">LM I:75</span>]; speech comes from the blood, as brought in our writings many times). When they are trained to speak words of truth every day — through them a very great victory is achieved: <span class="v">"from the mouths of babes and sucklings You have established strength, for the sake of Your adversaries — to silence the enemy and the avenger."</span></div>

<div class="p">Man's war in this world is very long — therefore one must begin from the days of youth itself: <span class="v">"it is good for a man to bear the yoke from his youth."</span> Would that he could complete it as Hashem desires, all the days of his life!</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"When I see Your heavens, the work of Your fingers, the moon and stars You have established — what is man that You remember him?"</span> All of this speaks of the moon's diminishment through the evil eye's provocation — which is itself the angels' complaint against man's creation and the giving of the Torah to the lowly. <span class="v">"When I see Your heavens... the moon and stars"</span> — the moon that was diminished (therefore given stars to console her, as our Sages say). When I see the diminished moon caused by the complaint from the evil eye — I wonder: <span class="v">"what is man that You remember him [<i>tizk'renu</i>]?"</span> — <span class="h">tizk'renu</span> specifically: how is it possible to draw the memory upon man, that he should remember his eternal purpose for the World to Come, when the evil eye overpowers so greatly that the moon was forced to diminish?</div>

<div class="p">But in truth: <span class="v">"You have made him a little less than G-d, and crowned him with glory and splendor."</span> Through His wondrous might, everything is transformed to good. As explained above — Hashem consoled the moon, saying that her diminishment touches Him Himself, as it were: <span class="mx">"bring an atonement for Me."</span> The deficiency touches His Name: <span class="v">"a hand upon the throne of Y-H"</span> — His Name is not complete and His Throne is not complete until the memory of Amalek is erased (Amalek being the essential evil eye, always jealous of Israel's greatness). Through Hashem joining Himself with the pain of the <span class="h">Shechinah</span> and the Congregation of Israel, swearing that His Name is not complete until Amalek is erased — they are assured they will certainly subdue him. He Himself hides Himself with us in the depth of our exile, through many concealments and contractions — and through this very thing He reminds us every day, every time, every place, every person, to return to Him. This is what is explained at the beginning of the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: Hashem contracts Himself from the Infinite to the endless depths, hinting to each person — even when one is in exile physically and spiritually, even if he has fallen, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>, to the endless depths. There too Hashem contracts Himself, hiding Himself through His wondrous ways, reminding him through many different hints every day and every place to return. <span class="v">"In all their troubles, He is troubled; the angel of His Presence saves them, in His love and compassion"</span> — in all Israel's troubles (the essential troubles being the soul's troubles: its distance from its Father in Heaven), then <span class="v">"He is troubled"</span> — He narrows, diminishes, and contracts His light through wondrous contractions to remind him wherever he is. And <span class="v">"the angel of His Presence saves them"</span> — the true <span class="h">tzadik</span>, called <span class="h">malach panav</span> [the angel of His Presence] (for the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> are called angels, as explained elsewhere). They understand the hints and strive in their wisdom to implant and illuminate these hints in Israel — thereby saving them, returning them to Hashem.</div>

<div class="p">Then everything is transformed to good — specifically through the evil eye's overpowering (diminishing and contracting the light greatly), through this very thing great good arrives: the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span> can elevate even those who have descended and fallen very far. Through the light being so concealed through the many contractions (making the hints difficult to perceive), the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> through their immense labor and self-sacrifice merited perceiving the light within the intense concealment. They attained great perceptions — with their essential wisdom, they clothe the light in wondrous contractions so that each person in his own place can receive hints, awakening, and counsel wherever he is, to hold onto Hashem always. The concealment is for the sake of the revelation (as brought in the writings of the holy Arizal <span class="h">zal</span>) — the more the light is concealed and contracted, the more it is transformed to good through the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>. Specifically through this, they reveal and illuminate for Israel the light of Hashem — so we can know and remember Him everywhere. This is: <span class="mx">"diminishment after diminishment — only to increase."</span> Every successive diminishment and contraction of the light is only to increase, magnify, and illuminate the light in Israel's hearts — specifically through this.</div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You have made him a little [<i>m'at</i>] less than G-d"</span> — <span class="h">vat'chas'raihu m'at</span> — the aspect of diminishment after diminishment, the light being diminished in many aspects. And it is <span class="h">mai'Elokim</span> [from G-d] — the deficiency and diminishment is from G-d Himself, as it were, His Name and Throne themselves having been diminished through the evil eye's provocation: <span class="v">"a hand upon the throne of Y-H."</span> But specifically through this: <span class="v">"and with glory and splendor You have crowned him"</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"You have clothed Yourself in majesty and splendor"</span> (explained at the beginning of the above <span class="h">Torah</span>): the feet of holiness (= the hints signaled to each person). Whoever merits understanding them merits great perceptions (= crown and diadem): <span class="v">"with glory and splendor You have crowned him"</span> — he merits being crowned with new <span class="h">mochin</span> drawn through glory and splendor (= the hints). He merits this specifically through the many contractions — through <span class="v">"You have made him a little less than G-d."</span></div>

<div class="p"><span class="v">"You have given him dominion over the works of Your hands"</span> — <span class="h">tamshilaihu</span> — the word <span class="h">mashal</span> meaning both parable and dominion. In all things in the world (= the works of His hands), He hints parables and hints so man can understand from them and draw close to Him. The hints are the aspect of parables: just as a parable is a circumlocution to convey something lofty that cannot be understood directly, so are the hints — Hashem clothing Himself in many garments and contractions so man understands from them parables and hints to draw close. This is also <span class="h">tamshilaihu</span> in its simple meaning of dominion — they are one. Man's essential dominion over all Creation (= the works of His hands) is when he merits understanding from everything parables and hints for His service. This is what Ben Azzai said: <span class="mx">"blessed is He Who created all these to serve me"</span> (as mentioned above). And: <span class="mx">"every person is obligated to say: the world was created for me"</span> — everything was created so I can understand from them hints to draw close to Him. This is what is written of Shlomo: <span class="v">"he spoke three thousand parables... he spoke of the trees, from the cedar in the Levanon..."</span> — understanding from everything hints (= parables). <span class="v">"Everything You have placed beneath his feet"</span> — everything is subdued beneath the feet (= the hints mentioned above, drawn from <span class="v">"the earth is My footstool"</span>). Whoever merits understanding these hints (= the feet of holiness) is encompassed in Hashem, and he is called the aspect of "His feet."</div>

<div class="p">Therefore it concludes: <span class="v">"Hashem our Master, how majestic is Your Name in all the earth"</span> — without repeating <span class="v">"You Who placed Your splendor upon the heavens."</span> For certainly it was right to create man and give the Torah — since specifically through the deficiency and diminishment he can strive to understand and attain such perceptions, until he can implant the memory of the World to Come in the heart of all Israel and magnify and make His Name majestic in all the earth: <span class="v">"Who raised all the ends of the earth"</span> (said at the end of the above <span class="h">Torah</span>). <span class="v">"Hashem our Master, how majestic is Your Name in all the earth"</span> — in all the earth, in all materiality, how majestic is Your Name — for the hints can be understood in everything through the power of the great <span class="h">tzadikim</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 17 — <i>Shabbos Nachamu</i>, <i>Tu B'Av</i>: The Sweetening of the Forty Days of Anger</div>

<div class="p">Returning to the matter of <span class="h">Shabbos Nachamu</span> — the Shabbos after <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span>, the consolation for the destruction of the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span>.</div>

<div class="p">The essential destruction is the forgetfulness that was drawn on the seventeenth of Tamuz through the breaking of the <span class="h">Luchos</span>. Therefore after <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span> one must begin rising before dawn, performing <span class="h">Tikun Chatzos</span>, and studying Torah — to guard the memory. As our Sages warn: <span class="mx">"from here on, whoever increases [in study] shall increase [in life]; whoever does not increase..."</span> The Sages gave the mnemonic: <span class="mx">"from my place — until I lie down; arise"</span> — from <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span> until <span class="h">Shavuos</span> one must fulfill: <span class="v">"arise, cry out in the night, at the beginning of the watches; pour out your heart like water before the face of Hashem."</span> The essential guarding of the memory (remembering the eternal purpose of the World to Come) is through this. All of this is preparation for <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — as we heard from his holy mouth: <span class="mx">"the essential thing is <i>Rosh HaShanah</i>."</span> Just as man must prepare himself all the days of his life for the great, awesome Day of Judgment of the future, so in particular he must prepare himself all year for <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> (the Day of Judgment). Especially one must begin preparation when one sees the days approaching — from the fifteenth of Av onward.</div>

<div class="p">On the fifteenth of Av is the sweetening of the middle forty days of anger (which began immediately after the seventeenth of Tamuz when the <span class="h">Luchos</span> were broken and forgetfulness was born — causing the destruction and bitter mourning of the <span class="h">Bain HaMetzarim</span>). After <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span> the sweetening begins — essentially on the fifteenth of Av. As our Sages say: on that day, in the final year, the dying of the wilderness generation ceased — they checked themselves from <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span> to the fifteenth of Av and found themselves all alive. The decree of the spies (which was on <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span>: <span class="mx">"you wept a weeping of futility"</span>) and the tragedy of the broken <span class="h">Luchos</span> (on the seventeenth of Tamuz through the sin of the Golden Calf) are one aspect — connected to each other: <span class="v">"on the day I visit, I shall visit"</span> — Rashi explains that the decree of the wilderness generation through the spies began then, from the sin of the Calf.</div>

<div class="p">Both tragedies are the flaw of memory. The sin of the Calf caused the breaking of the <span class="h">Luchos</span> — forgetfulness. The essential evil of the Calf was through the <span class="h">Erev Rav</span> (= the evil eye): they were Yunos and Yumbros, sons of Bilam the wicked, who had an evil eye (as our Sages say). They made the Calf (as the Zohar explains) — always having an evil eye toward Moshe's and Israel's greatness, until they overpowered with their evil eye and caused Israel to sin, causing the breaking of the <span class="h">Luchos</span> (= forgetfulness, death of the heart from the evil eye). The sin of the spies is the flaw of <span class="h">lashon hara</span> — causing the evil in the imaginative faculty to overpower (= fallen loves, which also flaw the memory, as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>).</div>

<div class="p">Through all this the <span class="h">Bais HaMikdash</span> was destroyed, from the seventeenth of Tamuz to <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span>. Afterward the sweetening begins. On <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span> itself, at the height of the bitterness of destruction, at <span class="h">Minchah</span> time, the sprouting of salvation's horn begins — then <span class="h">Mashiach</span> was born (as is brought), through whom the evil eye is essentially subdued. The essential sweetening is on the fifteenth of Av — when the dying ceased. Then the illumination of the Will begins to sparkle — Hashem became appeased with Israel during the last forty days (<span class="h">Rosh Chodesh Elul</span> to <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>), which were days of appeasement. At the end of the forty days of anger, Hashem was appeased with Moshe through his many <span class="h">tefilos</span> and said: <span class="v">"carve for yourself..."</span> This sweetening and Will began on the fifteenth of Av — sweetening the anger of the middle days. Therefore our Sages equated the <span class="h">Yom Tov</span> of the fifteenth of Av with <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>: <span class="mx">"there were no days as good for Israel as the fifteenth of Av and <i>Yom Kippur</i>."</span> <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> is the completion of the sweetening of the last forty days of appeasement — the full completion of sweetening, when Hashem was appeased with Moshe joyfully and said: <span class="v">"I have forgiven"</span> (as Rashi explains). But the beginning — when Hashem began to be appeased to give the second <span class="h">Luchos</span> on <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — began on the fifteenth of Av, when the sweetening and Will of the last forty days first sparkled. On that day the dying ceased (= one aspect with the breaking of the <span class="h">Luchos</span>). Therefore the fifteenth of Av is connected to <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 18 — Elul, <i>Shofar</i>, and <i>Yom Kippur</i>: Repairing the Memory Through the Second <i>Luchos</i></div>

<div class="p">Therefore immediately after <span class="h">Tisha B'Av</span>, especially on <span class="h">Tu B'Av</span>, one must begin repairing the flawed memory caused by the breaking of the <span class="h">Luchos</span>. Now one must draw upon oneself the illumination of receiving the second <span class="h">Luchos</span> — drawn during the last forty days until <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — through which the memory is repaired. Therefore one must then begin rising at midnight — the tikun of memory.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of Elul — when all Israel awaken and remember Hashem and the World to Come. Therefore we blow the <span class="h">shofar</span> — the <span class="h">shofar</span> being the aspect of memory: <span class="v">"it shall be for you as a remembrance"</span>; <span class="v">"if a <i>shofar</i> is blown in a city, will the people not tremble?"</span> Through the <span class="h">shofar</span> we remind ourselves of the awesome Day of Judgment of the future, when the great <span class="h">shofar</span> will be blown: <span class="v">"on that day the great <i>shofar</i> shall be blown."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential tikun of memory is drawn on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — the essential <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">shofar</span> — <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span> [the Day of Remembrance]. It is the first of the Ten Days of <span class="h">Teshuvah</span>; the memory must be drawn upon us — through which comes the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>. For this reason one must come then to the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> — the essential purpose of traveling to the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> is for the memory of the World to Come: that they remind him of his eternal purpose and teach him counsel and straight paths to merit it. Therefore one must be especially careful to come for <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — <span class="h">Yom HaZikaron</span> — when the true <span class="h">tzadikim</span> work most to draw the memory.</div>

<div class="p">On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span>, Adam HaRishon was created — his essential creation was to cleave to the Tree of Life and live immediately with the eternal life of the World to Come. But the <span class="h">Satan</span> and the serpent had an evil eye toward his greatness (as our Sages say: <span class="mx">"the angels were roasting meat and cooling wine for him"</span>) — through their jealousy they attacked him and caused him to sin. Therefore the essential flaw came to the eyes: <span class="v">"the woman saw"</span> — <span class="h">vatairah</span> [she saw] specifically; <span class="v">"the eyes of both of them were opened and they knew"</span> — our Sages say: regarding wisdom this is said. But in this wisdom the evil eye took hold — the wisdoms of the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil. Through this they knew they were naked and needed garments to cover and conceal themselves — all from having drawn the evil eye's hold into the world (before the evil eye's hold, they had no need of garments to conceal themselves). On <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> we engage in repairing the sin of Adam HaRishon (who sinned then) — therefore it is the great Day of Judgment. The essential work of the <span class="h">tzadikim</span> then is to subdue the evil eye (= Esav and his four hundred men, who seeks to rise then, as is brought) — through the <span class="h">shofar</span> (= memory). Memory is the opposite of the evil eye (= forgetfulness). This is the aspect of sleep and the awakening of sleep on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> — both requiring memory in general and in particular, as understood from the above <span class="h">Torah</span>.</div>

<div class="p">On <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> — the completion of the memory's tikun. Then the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> enters the innermost sanctum, to the place of the <span class="h">Luchos</span> — the essential completeness of the memory in man, place, and time in the ultimate completeness (as above). Then the second <span class="h">Luchos</span> were given — tikun of the breaking (through which forgetfulness came). Now the matter is repaired and the second <span class="h">Luchos</span> are given. Through this, the completeness of memory is drawn to every person, every place, every time — through the ultimate holiness of man, place, and time: the <span class="h">Kohain Gadol</span> entering on such a holy day into the <span class="h">Kodesh Kodashim</span>. [Only now it cannot be repaired in a single year — all generations must engage in repairing the sin of Adam HaRishon and the breaking of the <span class="h">Luchos</span> until the coming of <span class="h">Mashiach</span>.]</div>

<div class="sn">§ 19 — <i>Sukkos</i>: The Clouds of Glory Shield from the Evil Eye</div>

<div class="p">Therefore after <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, Hashem graced us with making <span class="h">Sukkos</span> — the Clouds of Glory that were in the merit of Aharon HaKohain, which covered us from the evil eye so it would no longer rule over us. The Clouds of Glory were in Aharon's merit and shielded Israel, covering them from the evil eye. When Aharon departed (on the first of Av): <span class="v">"the entire congregation saw that Aharon had expired"</span> — our Sages expound: do not read <span class="v">"they saw [<i>va'yir'u</i>]"</span> but <span class="v">"they feared [<i>va'yira'u</i>]."</span> After the Clouds of Glory departed, they became visible to the nations' eye — then the evil eye (= Amalek, the essential evil eye, always jealous of Israel's greatness) attacked them: <span class="v">"the Canaanite heard"</span> — this is Amalek, as Rashi explains. All this was on the first of Av — when the bitter days of the destruction begin.</div>

<div class="p">On <span class="h">Sukkos</span> (after <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, when we merited repairing all this), Hashem graced us with drawing the Clouds of Glory through the holy <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">Sukkah</span> (= the Clouds of Glory, as our Sages say) — to cover and shield us from the evil eye, so the holy memory drawn upon us during <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> would not be flawed, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. As the Zohar says regarding Esav and his four hundred men: Israel engage on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> in many strategies to subdue and distance him. And regarding the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">Sukkah</span>: <span class="mx">"then Yaakov journeyed to Sukkos"</span> — Esav and his four hundred (= the evil eye, numerically four hundred) are subdued through <span class="h">Sukkos</span> (= the Clouds of Glory that shield Israel from the evil eye's hold).</div>

<div class="p">Therefore we increase <span class="h">korbanos</span> on <span class="h">Sukkos</span> — the aspect of the hand [<span class="h">yad</span>]: <span class="v">"you shall rejoice in all the works of your hands"</span> (mentioned in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>). Through this the imaginative faculty is subdued. Since on <span class="h">Rosh HaShanah</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span> the memory's tikun was drawn, we now strive to subdue the evil eye through the <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">Sukkah</span> and to subdue the imaginative faculty through the joy of the many <span class="h">korbanos</span>: <span class="v">"you shall rejoice"</span> — for then is <span class="h">z'man simchasainu</span> [the time of our joy], so the memory is not corrupted, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of the <span class="h">Arba Minim</span> [Four Species] taken on <span class="h">Sukkos</span> — through which we draw upon ourselves the hints mentioned above. We wave them in every direction — the aspect of the hints, so we can recognize Him in every place and every time through the thought, speech, and action of each day (= the aspect of <span class="h">ADaM</span> — initials of <span class="h">Adam</span>, <span class="h">Dibur</span>, <span class="h">Ma'aseh</span> [man, speech, action], as brought in the <span class="h">kavanos</span>). The <span class="h">Arba Minim</span> are repaired through the circuits [<span class="h">hakafos</span>] — thought, speech, action (the essential of man being the thought in the mind = the soul). As the holy Zohar says: through taking the Four Species in our hands, we take Him, as it were, into our hands — drawing the hints (= the memory), what Hashem contracts Himself from the Infinite to the endless depths through the thought, speech, and action of each day, hinting to every person in every place and every day to draw close and return to Him.</div>

<div class="p">This is what the Zohar says: <span class="mx">"who is victorious? He who holds the weapons of war in his hand."</span> This seems puzzling — the opponent also holds weapons; is holding a weapon the victory? <span class="v">"Let not the one who girds boast like the one who removes [his armor]"</span> (Melachim I, 20). But based on the above, it is well explained. Whoever intends truth and girds himself for war for truth's sake — always wins. Every act he does for this holy war is an immediate victory, since his intention is truly for Heaven. <span class="mx">"Who is victorious? He who holds the weapons of war in his hand"</span> — these holy weapons (the <span class="h">esrog</span>, <span class="h">lulav</span>, etc.) are the Creator's <span class="h">mitzvos</span>. The instant we hold them in our hands, we are victorious — since our intention is to fulfill our Creator's <span class="h">mitzvos</span>, to draw the memory upon us, and to subdue the evil eye from causing forgetfulness. <span class="h">Didan natzach</span> [our side has won] — always, for only eternal victory is true victory.</div>

<div class="sn">§ 20 — Marriage: The <i>Chupah</i>, Joy, Veiling the Bride, and the Memory of the World to Come</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of marriage [<span class="h">chasunah</span>]. The essential purpose of marriage is for the memory — to leave remembrance after oneself forever. This is the essential <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of marriage (the first <span class="h">mitzvah</span> in the Torah): to leave children after him, that his memory endures for all generations. The essential purpose is the memory of the World to Come — the essential purpose of the world. For this alone Hashem desires the world's continuity — that man beget children so the world endures, remembering Him forever: <span class="v">"Hashem, Your remembrance is for all generations"</span>; <span class="v">"this is My remembrance for all generations"</span> — the memory of the World to Come. The essential memory of the World to Come is to remember Him (as understood from the above <span class="h">Torah</span>) — the essential delight of the World to Come, when all will know Him. Man was created in this world solely for this, and solely for this he is obligated to leave children — that they remember Him forever: <span class="v">"this is My remembrance for all generations."</span> As understood there: one who flaws the memory does not merit a male child (as brought there regarding Avshalom).</div>

<div class="p">The essential memory regarding the World to Come pertains to this desire [marriage] — one must remember the World to Come so as not to be ensnared, <span class="h">chas v'shalom</span>. This is the <span class="h">chupah</span> [canopy] made before the wedding — to remember the World to Come. The <span class="h">chupah</span> is the aspect of the World to Come: <span class="mx">"Hashem will make a <i>chupah</i> for every <i>tzadik</i> in the future"</span>; <span class="v">"for over all glory there shall be a <i>chupah</i>."</span> The <span class="h">chupah</span> is also the aspect of subduing the evil eye (= flaw of the memory of the World to Come): <span class="v">"<i>chofaif</i> [covering] over him all day"</span> — the <span class="h">chupah</span> covers and shields from the evil eye, preventing forgetfulness, always remembering the World to Come (which is the <span class="h">chupah</span>). The essential hold of the evil eye is through this desire, which depends on the eyes: <span class="v">"do not stray after your hearts and after your eyes."</span> Therefore the <span class="h">chupah</span> is made — to subdue the evil eye and shield against it.</div>

<div class="p">This is the great <span class="h">mitzvah</span> of <span class="h">simchas chassan v'kalah</span> [rejoicing with the groom and bride] — to subdue the imaginative faculty (from which come fallen loves = also a flaw of memory, as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>). Its subduing is through joy — the aspect of melody on the <span class="h">kinor</span> and musical instruments. This is the musical instruments played at the wedding — to clarify the good spirit from the evil spirit, subduing the evil in the imaginative faculty through the strengthening of joy — guarding the memory of the World to Come, always remembering that the essential marriage is for the World to Come: to leave remembrance that they remember Him forever.</div>

<div class="p">This is what our Sages extolled regarding the joy of <span class="h">Tu B'Av</span> and <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>: <span class="mx">"there were no days as good for Israel as the fifteenth of Av and <i>Yom Kippur</i>."</span> The essential joy is the aspect of marriage, as the <span class="h">Mishnah</span> says: <span class="mx">"on them the daughters of Israel would go out..."</span> and concludes: <span class="v">"go out and see... on the day of his wedding"</span> — this is the giving of the Torah. On these days the memory of the World to Come was repaired: the flaw of the broken <span class="h">Luchos</span> (= forgetfulness) was sweetened and repaired. Therefore the essential joy is the joy of marriage: <span class="v">"on the day of his wedding"</span> — the essential joy of marriage is the joy of the World to Come. Through this proper marriage according to the holy Torah, children of Israel are born who always remember the World to Come — His remembrance enduring from generation to generation forever: <span class="v">"this is My remembrance for all generations."</span></div>

<div class="p">The essential root of all joys is the joy of the World to Come — when <span class="v">"our mouths shall be filled with laughter"</span>; <span class="v">"eternal joy upon their heads"</span>; <span class="mx">"Hashem will make a dance for the <i>tzadikim</i> in the World to Come."</span> The instant one remembers the World to Come, each person should rejoice in his share — the good point of Israel's holiness within him. The merest spark of Israel's holiness in the lowliest of the lowly — the entire world is not worth bearing the immense joy he will merit in the future. Every person called by the name of Israel will merit <span class="v">"seven joys [<i>sova s'machos</i>]"</span> — joy so great it will satisfy and overflow each one, even the smallest of the small: <span class="v">"You shall show me the path of life — the fullness of joys [<i>sova s'machos</i>] before Your face; pleasantness at Your right hand forever."</span> <span class="h">Amain</span> and <span class="h">amain</span>.</div>

<div class="p">This is what is said there in the <span class="h">Mishnah</span>: the women would say: <span class="mx">"do not set your eyes on beauty — set your eyes on family."</span> Do not be drawn after the evil eye that looks only at beauty — at worldly desire. Rather, set your eyes on family — on the good forefathers who were <span class="h">tzadikim</span> and cleaved their thought all their days to the World to Come. Only for this do you need to marry: so the memory of the World to Come endures forever.</div>

<div class="p">This is the aspect of covering the bride's face — to subdue the evil eye, showing that the evil eye has no hold in the proper marriage of Israel. We conduct the marriage according to the Torah, specifically for the memory of the World to Come.</div>

<div class="p"><i>[Addendum relating to § 17:]</i> The sin of the spies is the flaw of memory through the evil report of the land (= <span class="h">lashon hara</span>), which flaws the imaginative faculty (= flawed memory), as explained in the above <span class="h">Torah</span>. This is why the <span class="h">parshah</span> of the libation-offerings [<span class="h">n'sachim</span>] is placed immediately after the <span class="h">parshah</span> of the spies. As explained there: the tikun and clarification of the imaginative faculty (= tikun of memory) is through joy — the aspect of: <span class="v">"he played with his hand and it was good for you"</span>; holy melody on instruments. The <span class="h">n'sachim</span> [libation-offerings]: when they were offered, the Levites would play their song on the platform — <span class="mx">"song is said only upon wine."</span> All the <span class="h">korbanos</span> from animals served to clarify the imaginative faculty (= the animal/bestial power, as understood from the above <span class="h">Torah</span>: <span class="v">"you shall rejoice in all the works of your hands"</span> — said of the <span class="h">korbanos</span>). The essential completeness of clarifying the imaginative faculty through <span class="h">korbanos</span> was through the song sung over the wine libation (= the essential joy: wine that gladdens). The essential tikun and clarification of the imaginative faculty is through joy from holy melody on instruments. Through this the memory is repaired: <span class="v">"we shall mention Your loves [<i>dodecha</i>] more than wine"</span> — <span class="mx">"remember it [<i>zachraihu</i>] over wine"</span> — said regarding <span class="h">Shabbos</span>, as our Sages expound on: <span class="v">"remember the day of <i>Shabbos</i> to sanctify it — remember it over wine."</span> <span class="h">Shabbos</span> is the aspect of the World to Come. The memory of <span class="h">Shabbos</span> (= the memory of the World to Come) is through wine (= joy): <span class="mx">"song is said only upon wine."</span> Therefore the <span class="h">parshah</span> of <span class="h">n'sachim</span> is placed next to the <span class="h">parshah</span> of the spies — the wine libation being the tikun of the memory that the spies had flawed.</div>

